above. Given that $c=a+\\lambda b,$ find $\\lambda .$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14041137/state.json" "file_sha256" "7b45918dce3886ca51db8028aa4826e89248726560585c047f25f73244c890e7" "basis" 1486 "verification" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14041137/edb-import/verification.json"} {"question" "q-19338" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "To find $\\lambda ,$ we want to match the expression\n\n$$\nc=a+\\lambda b.\n$$\n\nWe are given that one of the components is $a.$ So, we begin with the vector $a,$ and then find the remaining vector needed to reach the point $C.$\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14041137/assets/6782f2fa59056afd9022edbaf69c44a991bf12a7ac6a190bea3cc08fc7a4996c.png)\n\nNote that in $△OAC,$ we have:\n\n- $AC=2.5|b|$\n\n- $\\overset{\\to }{AC}$ and $b$ have the same direction\n\nHence, $\\overset{\\to }{AC}=2.5b.$\n\nAdditionally,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}c & =\\overset{\\to }{OA}+\\overset{\\to }{AC} \\\\  & =a+2.5b.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nConsequently, $c=a+\\lambda b=a+2.5b.$\n\nSince $a∦b,$ the linear combination can only be written in one way. Equating the coefficients of $b,$ we get:\n\n$$\n\\lambda =2.5\n$$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14041137/state.json" "file_sha256" "7b45918dce3886ca51db8028aa4826e89248726560585c047f25f73244c890e7" "basis" 1486 "verification" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14041137/edb-import/verification.json"} {"question" "q-19338" "field" nil "attribute" "question/difficulty" "value" :question.difficulty/easy "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/1404113