ourse_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14073957/state.json" "file_sha256" "c08025d4a49cc2cdf60b64859cbb3b2f43dcee6ee8e3852b1acaabc7b0947d6e"} {"question" "q-20435" "field" nil "attribute" "question/difficulty" "value" :question.difficulty/moderate "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14073957/activity-metadata.json" "file_sha256" "0357189807dde4a8c521cd09a69f210f341a5cfd8c7709433377b3ffa5416436"} {"question" "q-20435" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/type" "value" :answer-field.type/radio "category" "ma_widget" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14073957/state.json" "file_sha256" "c08025d4a49cc2cdf60b64859cbb3b2f43dcee6ee8e3852b1acaabc7b0947d6e"} {"question" "q-20435" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/choices" "value" [["math" "-2"] ["math" "1"] ["math" "2"] ["math" "\\frac{1}{2}"] ["math" "\\frac{3}{2}"]] "category" "ma_complete_choices" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14073957/state.json" "file_sha256" "c08025d4a49cc2cdf60b64859cbb3b2f43dcee6ee8e3852b1acaabc7b0947d6e"} {"question" "q-20435" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/correct" "value" [:answer.type/math "\\frac{1}{2}"] "category" "model_interpretation" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14073957/state.json" "file_sha256" "c08025d4a49cc2cdf60b64859cbb3b2f43dcee6ee8e3852b1acaabc7b0947d6e"} {"question" "q-275120" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14073957/assets/1d637a841739052b4e2d26f89bbcfd92eefefcebf6000a46a10a45b7e53ecf15.png)\n\nConsider the picture above. If $c=\\lambda a+3b,$ then$\\,\\lambda =$\n{{field-1}}." "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14073957/state.json" "file_sha256" "c08025d4a49cc2cdf60b64859cbb3b2f43dcee6ee8e3852b1acaabc7b0947d6e"} {"question" "q-275120" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "To find $\\lambda ,$ we want to match the expression\n\n$$\nc=\\lambda a+3b.\n$$\n\nWe are given that one of the components is $3b.$ So, we first draw the vector $3b$ from the origin. Then we complete the path to the point $C.$\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14073957/assets/33a5661bc10830c7e52e2ba7f27779a28c6e5f9cf39630e60d0634f3b6f7ccdd.png)\n\nLet $P$ be the endpoint of the vector $3b.$ Then\n\n$$\nc=\\overset{\\to }{OP}+\\overset{\\to }{PC}.\n$$\n\nIn $△OPC,$ we have:\n\n- $PC=3|a|$\n\n- $\\overset{\\to }{PC}$ and $a$ have the same direction\n\nHence, $\\overset{\\to }{PC}=3a.$\n\nAdditionally,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}c & =\\overset{\\to }{OP}+\\overset{\\to }{PC} \\\\  & =3b+3a.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nConsequently, $c=\\lambda a+3b=3a+3b.$\n\nSince $a∦b,$ the linear combination can only be written in one way. Equating the coefficients of $a,$ we get:\n\n$$\n\\lambda =3.\n$$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14073957/state.json" "file_sha256" "c08025d4a49cc2cdf60b64859cbb3b2f43dcee6ee8e3852b1acaabc7b0947d6e"} {"question" "q-275120" "field" nil "attribute" "question/difficulty" "value" :question.difficulty/moderate "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14073957/activity-metadata.json" "file_sha256" "0357189807dde4a8c521cd09a69f210f341a5cfd8c7709433377b3ffa5416436"} {"question" "q-275120" "field" "field-1" "attribute" "answer-field/type" "value" :answer-field.type/blank "category" "ma_widget" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14073957/state.json" "file_sha256" "c08025d4a49cc2cdf60b64859cbb3b2f43dcee6ee8e3852b1acaabc7b0947d6e"} {"question" "q-275120" "field" "field-1" "attribute