eveloper/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14073957/state.json" "file_sha256" "c08025d4a49cc2cdf60b64859cbb3b2f43dcee6ee8e3852b1acaabc7b0947d6e"} {"question" "q-327074" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14073957/assets/6708537b79154a7778fe52e8d82b6577f93d40a06d3af0267e3e4b7eaa6468a9.png)\n\nIn $△OMR$ shown above, we have \n\n- $OM=3|q|$\n\n- $\\overset{\\to }{OM}$ and $q$ have the opposite direction\n\nHence, $\\overset{\\to }{OM}=-3q.$\n\nSimilarly:\n\n- $MR=2|p|$\n\n- $\\overset{\\to }{MR}$ and $p$ have the same direction\n\nHence, $\\overset{\\to }{MR}=2p.$\n\nAdditionally,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}r & =\\overset{\\to }{OM}+\\overset{\\to }{MR} \\\\  & =-3q+2p.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nConsequently, $r=\\lambda p+\\mu q=2p-3q.$\n\nSince $p∦q,$ the linear combination can only be written in one way. Equating the coefficients, we get:\n\n- $\\lambda =2$ and $\\mu =-3$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14073957/state.json" "file_sha256" "c08025d4a49cc2cdf60b64859cbb3b2f43dcee6ee8e3852b1acaabc7b0947d6e"} {"question" "q-327074" "field" nil "attribute" "question/difficulty" "value" :question.difficulty/moderate "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14073957/activity-metadata.json" "file_sha256" "0357189807dde4a8c521cd09a69f210f341a5cfd8c7709433377b3ffa5416436"} {"question" "q-327074" "field" "field-1" "attribute" "answer-field/type" "value" :answer-field.type/blank "category" "ma_widget" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14073957/state.json" "file_sha256" "c08025d4a49cc2cdf60b64859cbb3b2f43dcee6ee8e3852b1acaabc7b0947d6e"} {"question" "q-327074" "field" "field-1" "attribute" "answer-field/correct" "value" [:answer.type/math "2"] "category" "ma_successful_grade" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14073957/state.json" "file_sha256" "c08025d4a49cc2cdf60b64859cbb3b2f43dcee6ee8e3852b1acaabc7b0947d6e"} {"question" "q-327074" "field" "field-2" "attribute" "answer-field/type" "value" :answer-field.type/blank "category" "ma_widget" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14073957/state.json" "file_sha256" "c08025d4a49cc2cdf60b64859cbb3b2f43dcee6ee8e3852b1acaabc7b0947d6e"} {"question" "q-327074" "field" "field-2" "attribute" "answer-field/correct" "value" [:answer.type/math "-3"] "category" "ma_successful_grade" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14073957/state.json" "file_sha256" "c08025d4a49cc2cdf60b64859cbb3b2f43dcee6ee8e3852b1acaabc7b0947d6e"} {"question" "q-120279" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14073957/assets/fc7d37da99173b7fa99f2f5f0bbf1478a54505628ed827488a18b3d01535f81f.png)\n\nConsider the picture above. Given that $l=\\lambda j+\\mu k$, find $\\lambda $ and $\\mu .$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14073957/state.json" "file_sha256" "c08025d4a49cc2cdf60b64859cbb3b2f43dcee6ee8e3852b1acaabc7b0947d6e"} {"question" "q-120279" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14073957/assets/6c9273c0b89d4c06c8a8591b8e8e94b0c83b2d1d4bd04b64ed8cb0a185619b5f.png)\n\nIn $△OJL$ shown above, we have \n\n- $OJ=|j|$\n\n- $\\overset{\\to }{OJ}$ has the opposite direction to $j$\n\nHence, $\\overset{\\to }{OJ}=-j.$\n\nSimilarly:\n\n- $JL=2|k|$\n\n- $\\overset{\\to }{JL}$ has the opposite direction to $k$\n\nHence, $\\overset{\\to }{JL}=-2k.$\n\nAdditionally, \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}l & =\\overset{\\to }{OJ}+\\overset{\\to }{JL} \\\\  & =-j-2k.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nConsequently, $l=\\lambda j+\\mu k=-j-2k.$\n\nSince $j∦k,$ the linear combination can only be written in one way. Equating the coefficients, we get:\n\n- $\\lambda =-1$ and $\\mu =-2$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14073957/state.json" "file_sha256" "c08025d4a49cc2cdf60b64859cbb3b2f43dcee6ee8e3852b1acaabc7b0947d6e"} {"question" "q-120279" "field" nil "attribute" "question/difficulty" "value" :question.difficulty/moderate "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14073957/activity-metadata.json" "file_sha256" "0357189807dde4a8c521cd09a69f210f341a5cfd8c7709433377b3ffa5416436"} {"question" "q-120279" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/type" "value" :answer-field.type/radio "category" "ma_widget" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14073957/state.json" "file_sha256" "c08025d4a49cc2cdf60b64859cbb3b2f43dcee6ee8e3852b1acaabc7b0947d6e"} {"question" "q-120279" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/choices" "value" [["text" "$\\lambda =-1$ and $\\mu =-2$"] ["text" "$\\lambda =-2$ and $\\mu =-1$"] ["text" "$\\lambda =1$ and $\\mu =-2$"] ["text" "$\\lambda =1$ and $\\mu =2$"] ["text" "$\\lambda =2$ and $\\mu =1$"]] "category" "ma_complete_choices" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14073957/state.json" "file_sha256" "c08025d4a49cc2cdf60b64859cbb3b2f43dcee6ee8e3852b1acaabc7b0947d6e"} {"question" "q-120279" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/correct" "value" [:answer.type/text "$\\lambda =-1$ and $\\mu =-2$"] "category" "model_interpretation" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14073957/state.json" "file_sha256" "c08025d4a49cc2cdf60b64859cbb3b2f43dcee6ee8e3852b1acaabc7b0947d6e"} {"question" "q-42676" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" 